proposaldescription:
The subject property is located at 11919 NE 128th Street and 12707 120th Avenue NE. On December 6, 2022, the Design Review Board (DRB) approved the Design Response Conference application for a 16-story mixed-use building consisting of 313 residential units and approximately 53,000 square feet of office space. Marc Boettcher, with Mainstreet Property Group, LLC submitted a request on February 26, 2026, to modify the DRB approved design.
The proposed modifications are summarized as follows:
•  Reduce the building height from 16 stories to 10 stories;
•  Reduce the number of residential units from 313 to 292;
•  Reduce the number of parking stalls from 430 to 292; and
•  Eliminate 53,000 square feet of office space, resulting in a fully residential building.

The proposed minor zoning code amendments include:
Aligning the list of zoning districts in the municipal code with the updated zoning map;
Allowing motor vehicle-oriented uses (such as sales, rental, service, and fueling stations) in the Community Commercial Mixed Use (CCX) and River Road Mixed Use (RMX) zones, where currently prohibited;
Allowing multi-family dwellings and townhouse developments (three or more attached units) to be developed as stand-alone residential projects in mixed use zones, consistent with recent state legislation;
Increasing maximum residential density in the RMX and Urban Center Mixed Use (UCX) zones;
Increasing the allowable size of certain exempt signs; and
Amending preliminary site plan procedures to establish a standalone administrative appeal process, addressing an identified gap where certain projects currently lack an appeal opportunity.

The City of Des Moines proposes to improve the storm drain systems along 4th Place S, 6th Place S, and S 287th Street through the Surface Water Capital Improvement Project (CIP) 37, with support from Flood Reduction Grand Funds from King County Flood Control District. The project would provide a flood reduction benefit that directly addresses existing flooding issues on 6th Place S near S 287th Street by replacing undersized infrastructure, constructing new conveyance lines, and installing a flow splitter to distribute storm flows more effectively. Project activities would include installing new and replacing existing storm drain pipes and catch basins; installing a new flow splitter; installing new asphalt berms;
trenching and restoring disturbed portions of 4th Place S, 5th Place S, 6th Place S, and S 287th Street, including replacement of pavement and curb, gutter, and sidewalk, where necessary. Two-inch grind overlay would occur at select locations along 6th Place s and 4th Place S. An existing gravel-and-wood stairway on the Redondo Ridge Condominium property would be replaced in kind, with no change in footprint. The project would include grading, filling, and clearing. Existing stormwater outfalls to Puget Sound would be used; the project would not create any new outfalls. 
The proposed flow splitter is a 54-inch drainage structure designed with an internal baffle weir. During most flow conditions, an outlet pipe would discharge to the existing ditch system, allowing low flows to continue using the established conveyance pathway. During high storm events, an overflow pipe would direct excess flows to a separate system that discharges to Puget Sound through an existing outfall. During the design storm, 3.4 cubic feet per second (cfs) would be bypassed to the separate system.

In 2009, the 13-lot subdivision (final plat) of Bridle Trail Estates was created (recording #200906035004).  In 2010, an alteration amended notes and moved/added tracts (recording #201003125002).  A second alteration now proposes the following:
•	Allow parcel #0121114004 (abutting the plat’s north side) to use the private roads in the plat;
•	From the private plat roads of 66th St. NW/Rosemont Dr. NW (Tract G/parcel #3001220190), create a new private road/ utility easement that would extend west across Lot 1 (parcel #3001220010) and Lot 13 (parcel #3001220130).  It would end at the southeast corner of parcel #0121114004;
•	On Lot 1, the new private road may cross an existing wetland/wetland buffer easement and on Lot 13 cross an existing stream buffer easement and open space easement; and
•	Remove the wetland/wetland buffer easement designation from north/east sides of Lot 1.
